library/real-analysis/sine-cosine-and-the-definition-of-pi.md:24:Sine and cosine are defined by their everywhere-convergent power series, rather than by geometric angle measure. Termwise differentiation gives the harmonic-oscillator system, whose initial-value uniqueness establishes the addition formulas. Parity and the Pythagorean identity follow algebraically, while elementary series estimates give positivity of sine and strict decrease of cosine on the interval where the first quarter turn is located.
library/real-analysis/sine-cosine-and-the-definition-of-pi.md-25-
library/real-analysis/sine-cosine-and-the-definition-of-pi.md-26-That first positive zero of cosine defines $\pi$. Quarter-turn shifts identify the first positive sine zero, then integer shifts give every zero and the least common period $2\pi$. The page records signs, monotonicity, ranges, the reciprocal trigonometric functions and their derivatives, and the fundamental limit $\sin x/x\to1$.
--
