research/audit/wave14-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-1-{"id":"def-real-exponential-function-and-e","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec_full.pdf","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_logandexp.html"],"rationale":"The MIT notes use the same globally convergent factorial power series for exp, and Lebl defines e=exp(1). The library packages those two standard conventions in one definition and makes the canonical-natural embedding explicit, so the content is semantically standard but not verbatim from one source. A definition supplies no proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl:2:{"id":"lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec_full.pdf"],"rationale":"The MIT notes state that the exponential power series has infinite radius and hence converges for every real argument. The local proof replaces their ratio-test presentation by an explicit geometric-tail comparison.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-3-{"id":"thm-exponential-addition-formula","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"literature-derived","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch10.pdf","https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec_full.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 10.24 states E(x)E(y)=E(x+y) for all real x,y and proves it by the same Cauchy-product and binomial-theorem calculation. The library's embedded-factorial notation is only a notational convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-4-{"id":"cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch10.pdf","https://math.mit.edu/~stevenj/exponential.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter states E(-x)=1/E(x) and E(x)>0 for every real x immediately after the addition formula; Johnson also derives reciprocal and positivity from the multiplicative equation. The local square argument is an adapted proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave14-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl-5-{"id":"thm-derivative-of-exponential","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch10.pdf","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_analfuncs.html"],"rationale":"Hunter gives E'=E from the factorial series, while Lebl's analytic-functions section gives termwise derivatives and the resulting smoothness of power-series sums. The item combines these into the stronger all-orders identity exp^(m)=exp.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
--
